[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Transaction Management and ACID Properties in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Database transactions are fundamental to enterprise Java applications. Hibernate, as an Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) framework, provides robust mechanisms to manage transactions and ensure data integrity through ACID properties."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding ACID Properties in Hibernate"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Atomicity: Ensures that all operations within a work unit succeed completely or the entire transaction is rolled back."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consistency: Guarantees that the database transitions from one valid state to another, maintaining all predefined rules and constraints."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Isolation: Determines how transaction integrity is visible to other concurrent users and systems."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Durability: Once a transaction commits, its results are permanent, even in the event of system failure."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Managing Transactions Programmatically"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hibernate utilizes the Transaction interface to manage transaction boundaries. Below is a standard implementation of programmatic transaction management using Session and Transaction APIs."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = null;
try {
    tx = session.beginTransaction();

    Employee emp = new Employee("John Doe", "Engineering");
    session.persist(emp);

    tx.commit();
} catch (Exception e) {
    if (tx != null) tx.rollback();
    e.printStackTrace();
} finally {
    session.close();
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Hibernate Transactions"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always scope transactions properly, keeping them as short as possible to reduce database lock contention."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle exceptions explicitly by checking for null transactions before executing a rollback."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer container-managed transactions (like Spring @Transactional) in enterprise applications over manual session and transaction handling."}]
